2. MIT License — code inherited from AgentNotch
================================================================================

Notchwatch is a fork of AgentNotch (https://github.com/AppGram/agentnotch).
Parts of this software are derived from it, and those parts remain under the
MIT License, held by the AgentNotch authors.

How that grant was made, stated exactly:

  The upstream repository contains no licence file. Its README declares the
  licence in a single line, quoted here verbatim:

      MIT License — see [LICENSE](LICENSE) for details.

  The file that line links to does not exist in the upstream repository; the
  declaration is the whole of the grant. What follows is therefore not a copy of
  an upstream file — it is the standard text of the licence that declaration
  names, set out here so that a recipient of Notchwatch has the terms in hand.

Copyright holders: the authors of AgentNotch. They are not named in the upstream
repository beyond its commit history, so no individual is named here either. Two
partial enumerations exist, and they do not agree. GitHub credits four accounts —
@nedimf, @serrrfirat, @0x8205 and @FlorianZimmer. The inherited commit history
(19 commits, 2026-01-07 to 2026-01-19; run `git log 4139d6f`) carries a fifth
author, james <james@bonkbot.io>, whose address is linked to no account and who
therefore appears in neither list above. The holders are the authors, whoever
they turn out to be; these lists are evidence about them, not a definition of
them.

